              "text": "Fossilized poop is known scientifically as a **coprolite** (from the Greek words *kopros*, meaning dung, and *lithos*, meaning stone). \n\nWhile it might sound like a joke, coprolites are actually some of the most valuable and exciting fossils in paleontology. They provide direct, undeniable evidence of what prehistoric creatures\u2014from dinosaurs to early humans\u2014were eating. \n\n---\n\n### Why Do Scientists Love Coprolites?\nBones and teeth tell scientists what an animal *could* eat, but coprolites tell them what the animal *actually* ate. By studying these fossils, \"paleoscatologists\" (scientists who study ancient poop) can discover:\n* **Diet:** Coprolites often contain tiny, fossilized fragments of food (called inclusions), such as crushed bones, fish scales, plant seeds, pollen, and wood. \n* **Health and Parasites:** Many coprolites contain the fossilized eggs of tapeworms, whipworms, and other intestinal parasites, giving scientists a look at the health of ancient animals.\n* **The Environment:** The pollen and plant matter trapped in the poop can tell scientists what the climate and plant life were like millions of years ago.\n\n---\n\n### How Does Poop Become a Fossil?\nFor poop to fossilize, it has to be buried very quickly before it decomposes, washes away, or gets eaten by dung beetles and bacteria. \n\nUsually, this happens in muddy or wet environments. Over millions of years, minerals (most commonly **calcium phosphate** or silicates) seep into the organic material, replacing it cell-by-cell and turning it into solid stone. By the time they are found, they have no smell and are completely made of rock.\n\n---\n\n### Famous Coprolites\n\n#### 1. \"Barnum\" (The *T. rex* Poop)\n* **The Creator:** Most likely a *Tyrannosaurus rex*.\n* **The Size:** Over 2 feet (67.5 cm) long, 6 inches wide, and weighing a whopping 20 pounds (9.28 kg).\n* **Why it's famous:** It holds the Guinness World Record for the **largest fossilized carnivore poop** ever found. Discovered in South Dakota in 2019, it is filled with crushed bones, confirming the *T. rex*'s ability to pulverize and digest its prey's skeleton. \n\n#### 2. The Lloyds Bank Coprolite (The Famous Viking Poop)\n* **The Creator:** A 9th-century Viking living in York, England.\n* **The Size:** 8 inches (20 cm) long and 2 inches (5 cm) wide. \n* **Why it's famous:** This is the **largest piece of fossilized human poop** ever found. Discovered in 1972 beneath a Lloyds Bank, it is so historically significant that one paleoscatologist declared it \"as precious as the Crown Jewels.\" It revealed that the Viking lived on a diet of meat and bread, but was also heavily infested with thousands of parasitic whipworms and maw-worms. Today, it has an estimated value of $39,000.\n\n---\n\n### Fun Facts about Coprolites\n* **The Poozeum:** There is an actual museum in Arizona called the **Poozeum**. It was founded by George Frandsen, who holds the world record for the largest collection of fossilized feces.\n* **The \"Lick Test\":** Geologists and paleontologists sometimes test if a rock is a fossil (like a bone or a coprolite) by licking it. Because fossilized bone and coprolites are porous, they will lightly stick to your tongue, whereas a normal rock will not. *(Yes, scientists really do lick ancient poop!)*\n* **They don't stink:** Because the organic material has been completely replaced by minerals, coprolites just smell like regular, earthy rocks.",
